{
    "title" : "DefaultLesson",
    "instructions": "WhoCares",
    "instructionsHTML": "<div class='FormattedTextSection'>\n      \n\n        \n    <section id=\"variables\">\n        <div class=\"part-header\">\n            <div>\n                <h2>Part 1: Variables &amp; Data Types</h2>\n                <p class=\"part-subtitle\">HOTDOGS,</p>\n                <p class=\"part-subtitle\">WHICH AREN'T SANDWICHES</p>\n            </div>\n        </div>\n\n        <h3>Variables — Sticky Notes for Your Data</h3>\n        <p>Y'know, it'd be very easy to realize that this was modified if you're <strong>NOT PAYING ATTENTION</strong>, but I feel like you'll notice it eventually, and then you'll eventually look over to the right and realize that this is over here as well.</p>\n\n          <div class='code-wrap'>\n        <div class='code-toolbar'><span class='code-lang'>Python</span><button class='copy-btn' onclick='copyCode(this)'>Copy</button></div>\n        <pre><code><span class=\"cm\"># Instead of memorizing 617-555-0192...</span>\n<span class=\"var\">best_friends_number</span> <span class=\"op\">=</span> <span class=\"str\">\"617-555-0192\"</span>\n<span class=\"var\">my_grade</span>            <span class=\"op\">=</span> <span class=\"num\">95</span>\n<span class=\"var\">passing_score</span>       <span class=\"op\">=</span> <span class=\"num\">70</span>\n<span class=\"var\">is_friday</span>           <span class=\"op\">=</span> <span class=\"bool\">True</span></code></pre></div>\n\n        <p>The <code>=</code> sign in Python <strong>does NOT mean \"equals\"</strong> like in math class. It means <strong>\"store this value under this name.\"</strong></p>\n\n<div class='code-wrap'>\n        <div class='code-toolbar'><span class='code-lang'>Python</span><button class='copy-btn' onclick='copyCode(this)'>Copy</button></div>\n        <pre><code><span class=\"cm\"># MATH class:  x = 5 means \"x and 5 are equal\"</span>\n<span class=\"cm\"># PYTHON:      x = 5 means \"put the value 5 in a box labeled x\"</span>\n\n<span class=\"var\">score</span> <span class=\"op\">=</span> <span class=\"num\">50</span>\n<span class=\"var\">score</span> <span class=\"op\">=</span> <span class=\"var\">score</span> <span class=\"op\">+</span> <span class=\"num\">10</span>   <span class=\"cm\"># This is TOTALLY fine in Python</span>\n<span class=\"cm\"># score is now 60 — math teachers, please look away</span></code></pre></div>\n\n        <div class=\"callout callout-fun\">\n            <div class=\"callout-title\"><span class=\"callout-icon\"></span>Naming Rules (Yes, There Are Rules)</div>\n            <ul>\n                <li>✅ <code>my_cat</code> — great!</li>\n                <li>✅ <code>player1_score</code> — awesome!</li>\n                <li>❌ <code>1player_score</code> — can\"t START with a number</li>\n                <li>❌ <code>my cat</code> — no spaces allowed</li>\n                <li>❌ <code>for</code> — that word is already taken by Python (called a <strong>keyword</strong>)</li>\n            </ul>\n        </div>\n\n        <h3>Data Types — Not All Values Are Created Equal</h3>\n        <p>Python needs to know <em>what kind</em> of thing you\"re storing. It\"s the difference between storing your age and storing your name — you wouldn\"t do math on your name (hopefully).</p>\n\n        <div class=\"table-wrap\">\n            <table>\n                <thead>\n                    <tr>\n                        <th>Type</th>\n                        <th>What It Is</th>\n                        <th>Example</th>\n                    </tr>\n                </thead>\n                <tbody>\n                    <tr>\n                        <td>int</td>\n                        <td>Whole numbers</td>\n                        <td><code>42</code>, <code>-7</code>, <code>0</code></td>\n                    </tr>\n                    <tr>\n                        <td>float</td>\n                        <td>Decimal numbers</td>\n                        <td><code>3.14</code>, <code>99.9</code>, <code>-0.5</code></td>\n                    </tr>\n                    <tr>\n                        <td>str</td>\n                        <td>Text (in quotes!)</td>\n                        <td><code>\"hello\"</code>, <code>\"Python rocks\"</code></td>\n                    </tr>\n                    <tr>\n                        <td>bool</td>\n                        <td>True or False only</td>\n                        <td><code>True</code>, <code>False</code></td>\n                    </tr>\n                </tbody>\n            </table>\n        </div>\n<div class='code-wrap'>\n        <div class='code-toolbar'><span class='code-lang'>Python</span><button class='copy-btn' onclick='copyCode(this)'>Copy</button></div>\n        <pre><code><span class=\"cm\"># INT — whole numbers only, no decimals allowed at this club</span>\n<span class=\"var\">num_homework_assignments</span> <span class=\"op\">=</span> <span class=\"num\">47</span>   <span class=\"cm\"># relatable pain</span>\n\n<span class=\"cm\"># FLOAT — for when life gets complicated (like GPA)</span>\n<span class=\"var\">my_gpa</span>            <span class=\"op\">=</span> <span class=\"num\">3.85</span>\n<span class=\"var\">pizza_slices_eaten</span> <span class=\"op\">=</span> <span class=\"num\">2.5</span>    <span class=\"cm\"># you know what you did</span>\n\n<span class=\"cm\"># STR — always needs quotes, single OR double, just be consistent</span>\n<span class=\"var\">my_name</span>        <span class=\"op\">=</span> <span class=\"str\">\"Alex\"</span>\n<span class=\"var\">favorite_class</span> <span class=\"op\">=</span> <span class=\"str\">\"Lunch\"</span>   <span class=\"cm\"># valid answer</span>\n\n<span class=\"cm\"># BOOL — only TWO possible values, the most decisive type</span>\n<span class=\"var\">homework_done</span> <span class=\"op\">=</span> <span class=\"bool\">False</span>   <span class=\"cm\"># we\"ve all been there</span>\n<span class=\"var\">is_weekend</span>    <span class=\"op\">=</span> <span class=\"bool\">True</span>    <span class=\"cm\"># 🎉</span></code></pre></div>\n\n        <h4>Type Conversion — Changing Costumes</h4>\n        <p>Sometimes you need to convert between types. Python won\"t do this automatically — you have to ask nicely.</p>\n\n<div class='code-wrap'>\n        <div class='code-toolbar'><span class='code-lang'>Python</span><button class='copy-btn' onclick='copyCode(this)'>Copy</button></div>\n        <pre><code><span class=\"cm\"># User input ALWAYS comes in as a string</span>\n<span class=\"var\">age_input</span> <span class=\"op\">=</span> <span class=\"fn\">input</span>(<span class=\"str\">\"How old are you? \"</span>)   <span class=\"cm\"># user types: 16</span>\n<span class=\"cm\"># age_input is \"16\" — a STRING, not a number!</span>\n\n<span class=\"var\">age</span>       <span class=\"op\">=</span> <span class=\"fn\">int</span>(<span class=\"var\">age_input</span>)   <span class=\"cm\"># NOW it\"s the number 16</span>\n<span class=\"var\">next_year</span> <span class=\"op\">=</span> <span class=\"var\">age</span> <span class=\"op\">+</span> <span class=\"num\">1</span>           <span class=\"cm\"># works! = 17</span>\n\n<span class=\"cm\"># Gotcha without conversion:</span>\n<span class=\"cm\"># \"16\" + 1  → ERROR (can\"t add string and number)</span>\n<span class=\"cm\"># \"16\" + \"1\" → \"161\" (string + string = joined together 😱)</span></code></pre></div>\n\n        <div class=\"callout callout-warning\">\n            <div class=\"callout-title\"><span class=\"callout-icon\"></span>Common Mistake Alert</div>\n            <code>input()</code> always gives you a string. If you ask someone their age and try to do math with it, Python will freak out. Always convert with <code>int()</code> or <code>float()</code> when you need numbers!\n        </div>\n\n        <h4>Quick Practice: Spot the Type!</h4>\n<div class='code-wrap'>\n        <div class='code-toolbar'><span class='code-lang'>Python</span><button class='copy-btn' onclick='copyCode(this)'>Copy</button></div>\n        <pre><code><span class=\"var\">a</span> <span class=\"op\">=</span> <span class=\"num\">100</span>          <span class=\"cm\"># int</span>\n<span class=\"var\">b</span> <span class=\"op\">=</span> <span class=\"str\">\"100\"</span>        <span class=\"cm\"># str  ← NOT the same as above!</span>\n<span class=\"var\">c</span> <span class=\"op\">=</span> <span class=\"num\">100.0</span>        <span class=\"cm\"># float</span>\n<span class=\"var\">d</span> <span class=\"op\">=</span> <span class=\"bool\">True</span>         <span class=\"cm\"># bool</span>\n<span class=\"var\">e</span> <span class=\"op\">=</span> <span class=\"fn\">int</span>(<span class=\"str\">\"42\"</span>)   <span class=\"cm\"># int (converted from str)</span></code></pre></div>\n\n    </section>\n    \n\n    <footer>\n        <p>Based on UAI Module 1 — Python Coding Basics &nbsp;|&nbsp; Ana Bell, MIT &nbsp;|&nbsp; Copyright MIT 2025</p>\n        <p >Teaching summary prepared for novice programmers 🐍</p>\n    </footer>\n\n</div>",
